WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.932
Reshape changes how values are arranged into dimensions without changing the sequence of underlying values.

00:00:08.932 --> 00:00:16.047
We begin with the numbers one through twelve in a one-dimensional array.

00:00:16.047 --> 00:00:21.407
Then we reshape those twelve values into three rows and four columns.

00:00:21.407 --> 00:00:28.242
Click Run and compare the original array, the matrix, and the matrix shape.

00:00:28.242 --> 00:00:31.062
The total number of elements must remain compatible.

00:00:31.062 --> 00:00:38.482
Twelve values can be reshaped into three by four, two by six, or four by three,

00:00:38.482 --> 00:00:44.102
but not into a shape that requires a different number of elements.

00:00:44.102 --> 00:00:49.507
The transpose, shown with dot T, swaps rows and columns.

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:53.327
A three-by-four matrix becomes four-by-three.

00:00:53.327 --> 00:01:00.437
Reshaping is useful when numerical data needs a specific structure for analysis, visualization,

00:01:00.437 --> 00:01:08.173
or machine-learning algorithms. Before reshaping, ask what each new dimension is supposed to represent.
